## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/de.svg" width="32" alt="Germany" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Germany

Germany achieves major trade diversification breakthrough through EU-India FTA while maintaining European strategic autonomy coordination without US participation in key diplomatic initiatives. Defense transformation continues positive personnel and procurement trajectory but confronts persistent capability gaps. The export-industrial model secures significant new market access (Indian automotive tariffs cut from 110% to 10%) while institutional innovation through E6 format demonstrates proactive European economic leadership. Domestic legitimacy constraints persist with AfD institutionalizing as governing alternative in eastern states (40% in Saxony-Anhalt) despite coalition policy achievements, while European framework coordination maintains collective capacity for managing alliance relationships amid continued US retrenchment.

**Key developments:**

- **Economic:** EU and India concluded comprehensive free trade agreement negotiations creating trading bloc of nearly 2 billion people. Agreement reduces tariffs on German automotive exports (from 110% to 10% for 250,000 vehicles annually), machinery, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals while protecting sensitive European agricultural sectors. Klingbeil called it 'new chapter of European trade policy' deepening strategic partnership with world's largest democracy.
- **Economic:** Klingbeil initiated E6 format with France, Poland, Spain, Italy, and Netherlands to strengthen European competitiveness amid geopolitical changes. First videoconference scheduled to accelerate European-level measures and increase ambition. Initiative reflects recognition that Europe must become stronger and more resilient in unpredictable geopolitical environment.
- **Economic:** Bundesbank President Nagel stated German gold reserves stored at Federal Reserve in New York remain secure despite alliance tensions over Grönland. Approximately 37% of Germany's €270 billion gold holdings are stored in New York, with Nagel defending the storage concept while acknowledging regular reviews of lagerstättenkonzept.
- **Diplomatic:** Merz delivered his first major foreign policy statement to the Bundestag calling for Europe to develop self-confidence and speak 'the language of power politics' in response to changing world order. He emphasized gestaltende Politik (shaping politics) and the need for Europe to learn the language of Machtpolitik while maintaining cautious optimism about crisis opportunities.
- **Diplomatic:** Wadephul traveled to Latvia and Sweden to discuss Baltic Sea security and coordinate action against Russia's shadow fleet, calling for stronger enforcement measures and expanded use of maritime law. Trip emphasized European unity against hybrid threats and need for effective sanctions implementation.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **CDU sparks controversy over plan to restrict right to part-time work** — The CDU's economic wing proposed limiting the legal right to part-time work to specific circumstances like childcare or eldercare, eliminating what they term 'lifestyle part-time work.' The proposal faces criticism from within the CDU and opposition parties. *([t-online.de](https://spiegel.de/politik/deutschland/spd-und-cdu-streiten-ueber-teilzeit-der-kampf-um-die-work-life-balance-a-075e1b0b-9eb9-40b7-ac0b-02aa581bc2f4))*
- **NATO's Rutte says Europe cannot defend itself without US assistance** — NATO Secretary-General Mark Rutte stated on January 26 that Europe would need to spend 10% of GDP on defense to defend itself without the US, calling such aspirations unrealistic. His comments sparked criticism from French officials and debate about European strategic autonomy. *([bild.de](https://www.spiegel.de/ausland/nato-europa-kann-sich-laut-mark-rutte-nicht-ohne-die-usa-verteidigen-a-15986f78-7283-47c0-84be-e2cb8c5f06ef))*
- **President Steinmeier attends Olympic opening ceremony and Holocaust remembrance events** — Federal President Frank-Walter Steinmeier traveled to Milan for the Winter Olympics opening ceremony and attended Holocaust Remembrance Day events on January 27, calling for vigilance against antisemitism and awarding sports honors. *([bietigheimerzeitung.de](https://www.tagesschau.de/inland/holocaust-gedenktag-steinmeier-100.html))*
- **UN faces potential bankruptcy as US withholds funding** — UN Secretary-General António Guterres warned of an imminent financial collapse of the United Nations, with the organization potentially running out of money by July 2026. The US owes several billion dollars in unpaid contributions. *([kettner-edelmetalle.de](https://www.zeit.de/politik/ausland/2026-01/vereinte-nationen-antonio-guterres-finanzkollaps))*
- **CDU economic wing proposes cuts to dental care coverage and social benefits** — The CDU-affiliated Economic Council proposed that dental treatments should no longer be covered by statutory health insurance, along with cuts to unemployment benefits and mother's pensions. The proposals face criticism from within the party. *([br.de](https://zdfheute.de/politik/deutschland/cdu-wirtschaftsrat-kuerzung-sozialleistungen-100.html))*
- **New Saxony-Anhalt Minister President Sven Schulze elected amid AfD pressure** — CDU politician Sven Schulze was elected as new Minister President of Saxony-Anhalt on January 29, replacing Reiner Haseloff. He faces the challenge of preventing an AfD victory in the September state election where polls show the far-right party at 40%. *([spiegel.de](https://mdr.de/nachrichten/sachsen-anhalt/ministerpraesident-sven-schulze-zukunft-landtagswahl-102.html))*
</Accordion>---

